program operational_information

-- =====================================================================
-- Operational information: what two sources know that neither knows
-- alone.
--
-- The story, for a reader who has never opened an information theory
-- book.
--
-- A hidden outcome is about to be revealed - call it the WORLD. You
-- must act before the reveal: pick one action from a fixed menu, and
-- pay a known loss that depends on which world turns up and which
-- action you picked. Before choosing you may consult sources. A
-- SOURCE is anything that narrows the possibilities: a sensor, a
-- report, a data feed. A COALITION is a set of sources consulted
-- together; what a coalition tells you about one concrete situation
-- is its OBSERVATION.
--
-- How much is a coalition worth? Not what it "contains" in the
-- abstract - what it saves you. Play the game blind and there is some
-- smallest expected loss you cannot beat, however cleverly you
-- choose. Play it with the coalition's observation in hand and there
-- is a new, usually smaller, smallest expected loss. The drop is the
-- coalition's DECISION VALUE. Information, measured operationally: the
-- expected loss it lets you avoid.
--
-- The reason this needs governing at all is the phenomenon the worked
-- data demonstrates. Take a secret bit computed from two others - the
-- exclusive-or, the parity, of A and B. Source A alone tells you
-- nothing about the secret: whatever A shows, the secret is still
-- fifty-fifty. Source B alone, the same. Consulted together they
-- determine the secret exactly. Each source is worthless; the pair is
-- decisive. The information exists only in the combination - so any
-- accounting that values sources one at a time and adds the figures
-- up gets this case exactly wrong. Whether a real portfolio of
-- sources behaves this way is a question of record, and this
-- programme is the record.
--
-- Who computes what. Searching for the best action is optimisation,
-- and optimisation stays outside: an external engine enumerates,
-- searches, does whatever it likes. But it cannot simply announce "the
-- best achievable risk is 0.5". It must file a CERTIFICATE: the
-- action it chooses at every observation, with each choice's exact
-- contribution to expected loss. The runtime recomputes every figure
-- from the admitted cases and refuses a certificate that is wrong by
-- any amount, or an action that any declared alternative beats. The
-- engine proposes; the record cross-examines. Nothing here trusts a
-- solver's word.
--
-- A note on words. The runtime never stores "facts". It stores CLAIMS
-- - statements someone has proposed and the rules have admitted. An
-- INVARIANT is a rule the runtime will not let any change break. A
-- `require` is a gate on a single action: a yes/no question asked
-- before that action may proceed.
-- =====================================================================


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The experiment and its parts.
--
-- An experiment names the game being priced and the BASELINE
-- coalition: the one that observes nothing, whose best risk is the
-- floor every other coalition is measured against. The baseline is an
-- ordinary coalition - it maps every case to the same observation, and
-- every rule below treats it like any other. No special case.
--
-- `unique by (experiment)` says the key determines the whole claim:
-- one experiment, one baseline. The runtime refuses a second,
-- contradicting declaration - the disciplines on every predicate below
-- read the same way.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

predicate Experiment(experiment: Subject, baseline: Subject)
  unique by (experiment)

predicate Coalition(experiment: Subject, coalition: Subject)

predicate Action(experiment: Subject, action: Subject)

-- One possible situation: which world it holds and how likely it is.
-- The probabilities are exact decimals proposed by the harness, and
-- the rules below hold them to account: never negative here, summing
-- to exactly one by the time the experiment seals.
predicate Case(experiment: Subject, case: Subject, world: Subject,
               probability: Decimal)
  unique by (experiment, case)

-- What a coalition sees in each case. Two cases mapped to the same
-- observation are indistinguishable to that coalition - that is the
-- whole notion of "knowing less". One observation per case per
-- coalition, by discipline.
predicate ObservedAs(experiment: Subject, coalition: Subject,
                     case: Subject, observation: Subject)
  unique by (experiment, coalition, case)

-- The price of ending up in `world` having chosen `action`.
predicate Loss(experiment: Subject, world: Subject, action: Subject,
               loss: Decimal)
  unique by (experiment, world, action)

-- Sealing closes construction. Before the seal the experiment is
-- being assembled; after it, cases, mappings, actions, and losses are
-- frozen, and certificates may begin. The completeness rules below
-- are conditioned on this claim, so sealing is the moment the whole
-- construction is judged at once.
predicate Sealed(experiment: Subject)

-- The certificate, in three layers.
--
-- A BayesChoice is one commitment: at this observation, this
-- coalition acts thus, and the choice contributes `weighted_risk` to
-- the coalition's total expected loss. The figure is the
-- probability-weighted loss summed over the cases behind the
-- observation - deliberately NOT divided by the observation's
-- probability. The division would change nothing (every action at the
-- observation shares the same denominator, so the same action wins)
-- and the undivided contributions add up directly to the total.
predicate BayesChoice(experiment: Subject, coalition: Subject,
                      observation: Subject, action: Subject,
                      weighted_risk: Decimal)
  unique by (experiment, coalition, observation)

-- A coalition's certified total: its Bayes risk, the smallest
-- expected loss it cannot beat.
predicate BayesRisk(experiment: Subject, coalition: Subject,
                    risk: Decimal)
  unique by (experiment, coalition)

-- And what the coalition is worth: the baseline's risk minus its own.
predicate DecisionValue(experiment: Subject, coalition: Subject,
                        gain: Decimal)
  unique by (experiment, coalition)

-- The pair under study: two member coalitions and their join. The
-- synergy read at the bottom of the file subtracts the members' values
-- from the joint value over exactly this claim.
predicate CoalitionPair(experiment: Subject, left: Subject,
                        right: Subject, joint: Subject)
  unique by (experiment)


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Construction rules: the experiment must be whole before it seals.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

-- A probability is never negative, from the moment it is proposed.
invariant probabilities_are_never_negative:
  Case(_, _, _, probability) implies 0 <= probability

-- At the seal, the case probabilities are a distribution: they sum to
-- exactly one. Not "close to one" - the comparison is exact, and a
-- harness that proposes 0.24 where 0.25 belongs finds the seal
-- refused.
invariant sealed_experiment_has_unit_probability:
  Sealed(experiment)
  implies sum(probability | Case(experiment, _, _, probability)) = 1

-- At the seal, every coalition accounts for every case. A case no
-- coalition observed is a hole in the game; the seal is refused until
-- the mapping is total.
invariant sealed_coalitions_observe_every_case:
  (Sealed(experiment) and Coalition(experiment, coalition)
   and Case(experiment, case, _, _))
  implies ObservedAs(experiment, coalition, case, _)

-- At the seal, every world that can turn up has a price for every
-- action on the menu. No un-priced outcome survives sealing.
invariant sealed_losses_price_every_outcome:
  (Sealed(experiment) and Case(experiment, _, world, _)
   and Action(experiment, action))
  implies Loss(experiment, world, action, _)

-- The referential floor: a mapping or a price may only name parts the
-- experiment has declared. These hold always, not just at the seal,
-- so construction proceeds declare-first.
invariant observations_map_declared_parts:
  ObservedAs(experiment, coalition, case, _)
  implies (Coalition(experiment, coalition)
           and Case(experiment, case, _, _))

invariant losses_price_declared_parts:
  Loss(experiment, world, action, _)
  implies (Case(experiment, _, world, _) and Action(experiment, action))


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Certificate rules: every figure is the record's own recompute.
--
-- These two rules are the heart of the example. The engine computed
-- its figures however it computed them; the record recomputes each
-- one from the admitted cases and refuses disagreement. Computation
-- and enforcement in different hands.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

-- A choice may only certify an observation the coalition can actually
-- see, with an action actually on the menu.
invariant choices_certify_declared_observations:
  BayesChoice(experiment, coalition, observation, action, _)
  implies (ObservedAs(experiment, coalition, _, observation)
           and Action(experiment, action))

-- The submitted contribution IS the recompute: probability times
-- loss, summed over exactly the cases this coalition maps to this
-- observation. The sum's target is the product itself - the quantity
-- expected loss is made of - and each matching case contributes its
-- own probability-times-loss exactly once.
invariant choice_risk_is_the_exact_recompute:
  let recomputed = (sum(probability * loss
    | Case(experiment, case, world, probability)
      and ObservedAs(experiment, coalition, case, observation)
      and Loss(experiment, world, action, loss)))
  BayesChoice(experiment, coalition, observation, action, weighted_risk)
  implies weighted_risk = recomputed

-- And the chosen action is unbeaten: for every declared action, the
-- same recompute with that action's losses does no better. Ties are
-- lawful - two equally good actions are both defensible - but a
-- strictly better alternative anywhere refuses the choice. This is
-- optimality as a checkable property of the record, not a solver's
-- status string.
invariant chosen_action_is_bayes_optimal:
  let alternative_risk = (sum(probability * loss
    | Case(experiment, case, world, probability)
      and ObservedAs(experiment, coalition, case, observation)
      and Loss(experiment, world, alternative, loss)))
  (BayesChoice(experiment, coalition, observation, _, weighted_risk)
   and Action(experiment, alternative))
  implies weighted_risk <= alternative_risk

-- A certified total is the sum of its parts: the coalition's risk is
-- exactly the sum of its choices' contributions, nothing added,
-- nothing dropped.
invariant coalition_risk_is_the_sum_of_its_choices:
  BayesRisk(experiment, coalition, risk)
  implies risk = sum(weighted_risk
    | BayesChoice(experiment, coalition, _, _, weighted_risk))

-- A risk certificate belongs to a declared coalition of a declared
-- experiment.
invariant risks_certify_declared_coalitions:
  BayesRisk(experiment, coalition, _)
  implies (Experiment(experiment, _) and Coalition(experiment, coalition))

-- A decision value is the risk given up: the baseline's certified
-- risk minus the coalition's own. The engine proposes the figure; the
-- record checks the subtraction.
invariant decision_value_is_the_risk_given_up:
  (DecisionValue(experiment, coalition, gain)
   and Experiment(experiment, baseline)
   and BayesRisk(experiment, baseline, baseline_risk)
   and BayesRisk(experiment, coalition, coalition_risk))
  implies gain = baseline_risk - coalition_risk

-- ... and its companions. The rule above compares against the
-- baseline's certified risk WHERE ONE EXISTS - a comparison rule goes
-- quiet when the claim it compares against is missing. These two
-- guarantee the claims exist, so the rule above can never pass
-- emptily. Rules that select a governing claim only govern as a pair
-- with the rule that guarantees the claim.
invariant decision_values_rest_on_a_certified_risk:
  DecisionValue(experiment, coalition, _)
  implies BayesRisk(experiment, coalition, _)

invariant decision_values_rest_on_a_certified_baseline:
  (DecisionValue(experiment, _, _) and Experiment(experiment, baseline))
  implies BayesRisk(experiment, baseline, _)

-- The pair under study names declared coalitions.
invariant pairs_name_declared_coalitions:
  CoalitionPair(experiment, left, right, joint)
  implies (Coalition(experiment, left) and Coalition(experiment, right)
           and Coalition(experiment, joint))


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The only ways state may change.
--
-- Construction first: each declaration passes its gates while the
-- experiment is open, and none is possible after the seal. There is
-- no transformation that retracts a case or a price - an experiment
-- is assembled, sealed, and priced; it is never quietly edited.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

transformation create_experiment(experiment_id, baseline_id):
  require not Experiment(experiment_id, _)
  admit Experiment(experiment_id, baseline_id)
  admit Coalition(experiment_id, baseline_id)

transformation declare_coalition(experiment_id, coalition_id):
  require Experiment(experiment_id, _)
  require not Sealed(experiment_id)
  admit Coalition(experiment_id, coalition_id)

transformation declare_action(experiment_id, action_id):
  require Experiment(experiment_id, _)
  require not Sealed(experiment_id)
  admit Action(experiment_id, action_id)

transformation declare_case(experiment_id, case_id, world_id, probability):
  require Experiment(experiment_id, _)
  require not Sealed(experiment_id)
  admit Case(experiment_id, case_id, world_id, probability)

transformation map_observation(experiment_id, coalition_id, case_id, observation_id):
  require not Sealed(experiment_id)
  admit ObservedAs(experiment_id, coalition_id, case_id, observation_id)

transformation declare_loss(experiment_id, world_id, action_id, loss):
  require not Sealed(experiment_id)
  admit Loss(experiment_id, world_id, action_id, loss)

transformation declare_pair(experiment_id, left_id, right_id, joint_id):
  require Experiment(experiment_id, _)
  admit CoalitionPair(experiment_id, left_id, right_id, joint_id)

-- The seal itself. The gate asks only that the experiment exists and
-- is not already sealed; the hard questions - unit probability, total
-- observation, total pricing - are the sealed-conditioned invariants
-- above, all judged at this commit. An incomplete experiment does not
-- seal, and the refusal names the rule that caught it.
transformation seal_experiment(experiment_id):
  require Experiment(experiment_id, _)
  require not Sealed(experiment_id)
  admit Sealed(experiment_id)

-- Certification: only a sealed experiment is priced, so the cases a
-- certificate is recomputed from can no longer shift under it.
transformation submit_choice(experiment_id, coalition_id, observation_id,
                             action_id, weighted_risk):
  require Sealed(experiment_id)
  admit BayesChoice(experiment_id, coalition_id, observation_id,
                    action_id, weighted_risk)

-- A coalition's total may only be certified once every observation
-- the coalition can see has a certified choice. The gate is the
-- completeness question, margin-call shaped: is there any observation
-- of this coalition still missing its choice? One missing observation
-- refuses the total.
transformation certify_coalition_risk(experiment_id, coalition_id, risk):
  require Sealed(experiment_id)
  require not (exists observation:
    ObservedAs(experiment_id, coalition_id, _, observation)
    and not BayesChoice(experiment_id, coalition_id, observation, _, _))
  admit BayesRisk(experiment_id, coalition_id, risk)

transformation certify_decision_value(experiment_id, coalition_id, gain):
  bind Experiment(experiment_id, baseline)
  require BayesRisk(experiment_id, baseline, _)
  require BayesRisk(experiment_id, coalition_id, _)
  admit DecisionValue(experiment_id, coalition_id, gain)


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The read side.
--
-- Two views, recomputed from the certificates whenever someone asks,
-- never stored on the side - so neither can drift from the claims it
-- is built from.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------

-- One row per certified coalition: its risk, and the gain recomputed
-- against the baseline's certified risk rather than read from the
-- stored DecisionValue - a summary that cannot disagree with the
-- arithmetic it summarises.
predicate InformationSummary(experiment: Subject, coalition: Subject,
                             risk: Decimal, gain: Decimal)

derived InformationSummary(experiment, coalition, risk):
  over (Coalition(experiment, coalition)
        and BayesRisk(experiment, coalition, risk)
        and Experiment(experiment, baseline)
        and BayesRisk(experiment, baseline, baseline_risk))
  value gain = baseline_risk - risk

-- The headline: how far the pair's joint value exceeds the sum of its
-- members' values. For the parity data this is the whole story - both
-- members are worth nothing, the pair is worth everything, and the
-- excess is the joint value itself. An additive excess witness, not a
-- full decomposition of who contributed what.
predicate PairSynergy(experiment: Subject, left: Subject,
                      right: Subject, joint: Subject, excess: Decimal)

derived PairSynergy(experiment, left, right, joint):
  over CoalitionPair(experiment, left, right, joint)
  value excess = (value DecisionValue(experiment, joint, _)
                  - value DecisionValue(experiment, left, _)
                  - value DecisionValue(experiment, right, _))


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
-- What this example deliberately does not cover.
--
-- Correcting a certificate after admission (supersession, as the
-- verified-revenue example models it); entropy, logarithms, or any
-- information measure beyond decision loss; and optimisation itself -
-- the search for good actions stays outside, because a record that
-- searched would have opinions, and the whole design is that it only
-- ever checks. It certifies finite expected-loss arithmetic over a
-- governed case corpus; it proves no general theorem about
-- information. What it does guarantee: no certified figure in this
-- record can disagree with the cases it was computed from.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
